You already have the key, so this is just installing the public half on fremont.

In PowerShell:

powershell
type $env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub | ssh fremont "mkdir -p ~/.ssh && chmod 700 ~/.ssh && cat >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys && chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys"

It'll prompt for your fremont password one last time.

Then add the key to your config so SSH offers it. Open C:\Users\y84451117\.ssh\config and make sure the fremont block has:

    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

Your a5 block already has it; fremont's was missing it.

Then test:

powershell
ssh fremont

Should go straight in. If it still asks for a password, check permissions on fremont:

bash
ls -ld ~ ~/.ssh
ls -l ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Home must not be group or world writable, .ssh needs 700, authorized_keys needs 600. Fix with chmod 700 ~/.ssh; chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys; chmod go-w ~.

Note this doesn't change the ncat proxy, that's separate and stays as is. And your fremont password is still in that config file in plaintext for the proxy auth, which is worth dealing with separately.
